Thanks a lot!

I attached a model output which is copy pasted from R. Deleted the model 't*sex' as 't+sex' is there.

Problem with the sigma is now solved. Mistake I made was that I transformed it as:

male sigma = exp(intercept)+exp(male)  ...

Now after correct transformation sigma in meters is 234.5 m for females and 114.5 m for males in the top model (which is necessarily not as expected for the white-tailed deer but still).

Just to make sure, in models with time and sex dependent p0;

p0.intercept is for females at occasion 1 (two occasions in my case) and p0.t2 would then be for females at occasion 2.

Then for males:

p0.t1 = exp(p0.intercept + p0.males)

p0.t2= exp(p0.t2 + p0.males)

I have understood that exp() should be used for this transformation but Daniel suggested plogis() which gives different results?

But how to interpret and transform psi? I always get values which are out of the range between 0-1..

thanks,

Jenni

Lähettäjä: Chris Sutherland <csuth...@umass.edu>
Lähetetty: 8. toukokuuta 2017 19:43
Vastaanottaja: 'Jeffrey Royle'; Jenni Poutanen
Kopio: 'Daniel Linden'
Aihe: RE: Problem with oSCR

The oscr group is bouncing my emails!

The estimates from the sex specific sigma model are for the linear predictor. So, for example, if the male sigma was double that of females, then the female sigma and the male EFFECT would be similar, so when you exponentiate the parameters they are similar. But, what you should do is add the male effect to the intercept (female).

So for the sex specific siga model:

female sigma = exp( intercept )

male sigma = exp( intercept + male )

Also note that the ‘t*sex’ detection model is exactly the same as the ‘t+sex’ model. That’s because this is a very unstable model and we didn’t implement it I think. I thought I had a warning in there to that effect.

Thanks,

Chris

